Hello folks, so today I came up with a very interesting question. It is related to topics like Dynamic Programming, greedy, etc. When i first encountered the ques i was damn sure that it can be done with the help of dp and the solution i came up with this one.

Using DP (TC O(n))
class Solution {
public:
    bool canJump(vector<int>& nums) {
        int n = nums.size();

        vector<bool> dp(n+1 ,false);
        dp[n-1] = true;

        for(int i = nums.size()-1 ; i>=0 ;i--){
            for(int j = 0 ; i+j<dp.size() && j<= nums[i] ; j++){
                if(dp[i+j] == true){
                    dp[i] = true;
                    break;
                }
            }
        } 
        return dp[0]; 
    }
};

** It is a good solution, but then I found one more approach in the comment section, and it was mindblowing, solving it in only O(n) time using Greedy.
class Solution {
public:
    bool canJump(vector<int>& nums) {
        int size = nums.size();
        int maxreach = 0;

        for(int i = 0 ; i < nums.size() ;i++){
            if( i > maxreach){
                return false;
            }    

            maxreach = max( maxreach ,  i +  nums[i]);
        }
        return true;
    }
};

The only intuition behind this solution is that the current index cannot be greater than the maxreach index. ex-> if maxreach index is 4 and we are at the 5th index, then tell how it is possible, we cannot reach till the 5th, maxreach is 4th. 

